/// Coarse product-line category for a position/asset `inst_type` raw string.
/// 
/// Kotlin shells used to branch on raw `inst_type` strings
/// (`instType == "OPTION"`, `instType == "SWAP" || instType == "FUTURES"`,
/// `instType == TRADE_MARGIN_TYPE`, `instType == TRADE_EVENTS_TYPE`, …) scattered
/// across the positions/assets Pure fragments. That is business classification
/// living in the native layer (rule A1). Instead, Rust classifies the raw
/// `inst_type` once via [`classify_inst_type`] and Native dispatches on this
/// flat enum — no raw-string comparison and no `com.okinc.unify_trade.constants`
/// import on the Kotlin side.
/// 
/// The variants mirror the five `instType` wire values produced by the backend
/// (`SPOT` / `MARGIN` / `SWAP` / `FUTURES` / `OPTION` / `EVENTS`); `Unknown`
/// covers any unrecognized value so Native always has a defined fall-through.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum OktInstTypeCategory {
    Spot,
    Margin,
    Swap,
    Futures,
    Option,
    Events,
    Unknown,
}

